Z||000000||V8Dreamer||05-28-2001||10:09 PM||||My 86 2m4 was rescued from the boneyard about 4 years ago.  It needed rings and had two broken shift forks.  Anyway, the rings were replaced and it was beaten before they could seal properly (no I didn't do it) and now it smokes like .....  I don't plan on replacing the rings anytime soon, does anyone have any suggestions from experience on how I can keep the smoking at a minimum?  You know it's gettin bad when you stop at a red light and your exhaust catches up with you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanks,<BR>Ryan ||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||05-28-2001||10:26 PM||||I know GM had a bulletin out for updated exhaust valve seals. Up grading them from the factor o ring style to an umbrella style.  The o ring style was said to cause blue smoke after prolonged idle and initial accell after prolonged idle.||||reg||1
Z||000002||V8Dreamer||05-28-2001||10:28 PM||||Well, those are the exact symptoms I am having, but I'm looking more for the mechanic in a bottle fix for now, like maybe some additive or switching to another brand of oil, like the Valvoline maxlife stuff??<P>Thanks,<BR>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||05-28-2001||10:34 PM||||Try running thicker oil and there is an additive I have set aside for a friend of mine with this concern.  is made by WYNNS and is like a "no smoke" in a can kind of treatment.  Chemicals-usually get what you pay for so don't buy the cheap chemical and be disappointed that it does nothing.<P>Valve seals on a duke is not a hard job for any garage.  Just req umbrella type seals.<p>[This message has been edited by PBJ (edited 05-28-2001).]||||reg||1
